// Canonicalising an expression, mirroring idc.py's _canon_expr.
//
// The canonical string of a function is what decides whether two functions are
// the same function written twice: they collide when their canonical strings
// are equal. So everything that makes two functions genuinely differ has to
// survive into the string verbatim -- operators, literals, the names of called
// functions, of imported globals and of exported variables -- while everything
// that is only a matter of spelling, namely the function's own parameters and
// locals, is replaced by the position at which the name was first met (see
// cn() in ../func/names.id).
//
// Nothing here reads a type or asks anything of a backend: the canonical form
// is a property of the parsed program, so the rule means the same thing
// whether the program is later emitted as C, as Python or as LLVM IR.
//
// The dispatch is an eager chain -- each link asks the next for a default and
// then overrides it for the two kinds it owns. Only the link that recognises
// the node descends into children, so a node still costs one walk of the
// chain and no more.

// A literal fingerprints by value, not spelling: the lexer already turns hex
// and binary spellings into decimal digits (compiler/lex/chars/numlit), so
// the only spelling left to normalise here is a decimal literal's leading
// zeros -- `007` and `7` are the same value, reusing the same strip_zeros the
// parser uses to size a literal (../../../front/parse/expr/atom/lit/int/is_bigdec.id).
canon_expr(int id) {
  string out = ce2(id);
  if(k_of(id) == "int") {
    string spell = s1_of(id);
    out = "I" + strip_zeros(spell);
  }
  if(k_of(id) == "float") {
    out = "F" + s1_of(id);
  }
} return string out;
given un_fixture ((import ev_node)):("I7")
given un_fixture ((import neg_node)):("u-(I7)")

ce2(int id) {
  string out = ce3(id);
  if(k_of(id) == "str") {
    out = "S" + s1_of(id);
  }
  if(k_of(id) == "var") {
    out = canon_var(id);
  }
} return string out;

// An imported global keeps its name: two functions that differ only by which
// global they read are different functions.
ce3(int id) {
  string out = ce4(id);
  if(k_of(id) == "import") {
    out = "g(" + s1_of(id) + ")";
  }
  if(k_of(id) == "bin") {
    out = canon_bin(id);
  }
} return string out;
